title The box held a day's instruments
prompt The baton -- Bakery's lane, core infrastructure, Lindy-first and crux-first, reds first.
rota lap 4144, row 4 -- Earth, the row that breathes in; the concrete fact taken in whole at the door, ahead of any argument. It paid twice this lap, both times by overturning something I had already reasoned my way into believing.
think Opened the round, started a cold --scoped roster pass in the background, and read the card and the Earth row while it ran.
think My own last recommend named the thread: width_check at 75s and crushed_index at 59s, the same question that had just paid on declared_ceiling -- what does the loop fork for before it skips. I went looking for that shape and found it in crushed_index: a per-page loop spawning awk, grep and head for every living Markdown page, then discarding almost all of them; and a signature loop running a full grep over every tracked tools file once per name.
obs EARTH PAID FIRST BY REFUSING MY ESTIMATE. I had the second loop written up as far worse than the first. Measured rather than reasoned: living_pages=512, not the ~1,800 I had carried over from another guard's corpus, and signature_names=1, so the full-corpus grep runs exactly once. The expensive-looking loop was the cheap one. The concrete fact outranks the plausible story, which is the row's own sentence.
obs THEN THE LEDGER NOBODY READS. construction/standing-equipment-hitrate.kyri has recorded 61 opens; a grep for its name across the tree finds the runner that writes it, one REDS row, and two session logs -- no scan, no witness, no reader. Read by hand: 54 miss, 6 none, 1 match. The digest-match reading it was built to feed has hit 1.6% of opens, because the digest covers HEAD and every lap commits. The door that actually saves the seconds is --scoped off the receipt's head, and the ledger records nothing about it.
think I was about to build that reader when reds-first sent me to the cold pass's own two reds, and the second measurement overturned the first.
obs THE BOX HELD A DAY'S INSTRUMENTS AND I WAS TWENTY MINUTES FROM BUILDING THEM AGAIN. stash_record was red at unlanded=2. stash@{0}, stashed by my own round-open at 051520, carried a complete green lap: standing_equipment_scope_rank.sh with 56 control behaviors, index_shelf_repair.sh with 24, the shared scope_match.sh matcher, a third gate on the map, a rostered witness, and the shelf-map row CLOSED (`20260907.045528`). Its scan is DIFFUSER's item 1 -- rank the map by cost x (1 - touch_rate) -- which is the instrument I had spent the morning re-deriving by hand from the same cost table.
think The recovery re-made the exact fault the recovered tool repairs. git stash apply merged two laps' rows into session-logs/date/README-index-20260907.md and left 043033 standing above 050056 -- %440's fifteenth firing. index_shelf_repair.sh sorted it and proved the result a permutation of the input before writing: first resident on its own first day, and a stronger green than any pen leg.
think stash@{2} wanted a different repair, and reading it before applying it is what kept the tree whole. Its scan and control were an ELDER line of work on reds_spine_derive whose descendants had already landed as e41294864 and 9766e92d1; applying the stash would have regressed the file it improved. Only its session log had never landed -- neither of those two commits carried one -- so the log alone was the repair, and every other path in that stash stays an orphan the guard reports rather than gates.
obs A LAP ENDS AT THE COMMIT, AND NOTHING REFUSES A LAP THAT ENDS AT THE STASH. The runner refuses a cold open over a dirty index under run_verdict=lap_unclosed, which catches the lap that stops after git add. A lap that stops just before the commit leaves a clean tree, gets stashed by the next round-open, and is caught only by stash_record on a later pass -- by which time the next open has buried it one deeper. Named on the card rather than taken.
